Here's the shape. I've deliberately left `Action::Log` *out* — see the note after.

```rust
use super::*;

/// NewSession -> container boots -> prompt buffered -> ready -> handshake -> flush.
#[test]
fn buffers_a_prompt_until_the_handshake_completes() {
    // Fresh session: no stored acp_session_id, so the handshake mints one.
    let mut core = SessionCore::new(AgentSessionId::TEST_A, None);

    // A prompt arrives while the container is still booting. Nothing on the wire.
    let actions = core.on_send(Some(user()), prompt("fix the flaky test"));
    assert_eq!(actions, vec![]);
    assert!(matches!(core.state(), SessionState::Booting { .. }));

    // Container reports ready. AcpReady means "handshakeable", not "sendable":
    // initialize goes out, the prompt stays buffered.
    let actions = core.on_inbound(event(SystemEvent::AcpReady));
    assert_eq!(actions, vec![Action::Send(initialize(req(1)))]);
    assert!(matches!(core.state(), SessionState::Handshaking { .. }));

    // initialize answered -> session/new. Still buffered: no acp session id yet.
    let actions = core.on_inbound(response(req(1), json!({ "protocolVersion": 1 })));
    assert_eq!(actions, vec![Action::Send(session_new(req(2)))]);

    // session/new answered -> id recorded, then the buffer drains.
    let actions = core.on_inbound(response(req(2), json!({ "sessionId": "acp-abc" })));
    assert_eq!(
        actions,
        vec![
            Action::SetAcpSessionId(AcpId::new("acp-abc")),
            Action::Send(prompt("fix the flaky test")),
        ]
    );
    assert_eq!(core.acp_session_id(), Some(&AcpId::new("acp-abc")));

    // Live: sends now pass straight through.
    let actions = core.on_send(Some(user()), prompt("and run clippy"));
    assert_eq!(actions, vec![Action::Send(prompt("and run clippy"))]);
}
```

Helpers the test needs — all trivial constructors, no mocks:

```rust
fn user() -> MacroUserIdStr<'static>;
fn req(n: u64) -> RequestId;
fn event(e: SystemEvent) -> ToServerMessage;
fn response(id: RequestId, result: serde_json::Value) -> ToServerMessage;
fn prompt(text: &str) -> ToRuntimeMessage;
fn initialize(id: RequestId) -> ToRuntimeMessage;
fn session_new(id: RequestId) -> ToRuntimeMessage;
```

## Two things this sketch decided for us

**Logging isn't an `Action`.** I tried it with `Action::Log(..)` in every vector and it drowned the assertions — every step became half log noise. Leaving it out is cleaner, but it's a real decision, not just cosmetics: **the pump then owns logging**, and the pump only sees what crosses the wire. So a buffered prompt gets logged at *flush*, not at enqueue. That answers hole #4 as "the log is an honest wire transcript" — and the cost is that a crash while a prompt sits in the buffer loses it silently. If you want the durable-buffer behavior instead, `Log` has to come back as an action and these assertions get noisier.

**`req(n)` implies the core mints request ids from a counter it owns.** That's what makes `initialize(req(1))` assertable at all. Worth being deliberate about — it means `SessionCore` is the only thing allowed to originate ACP requests on this session.

Want me to write this as a real failing test plus the type skeleton, or do you want to argue the logging call first?

Right — that pulls in the "don't pump, drive manually" hook and makes the log assertable. Note `ToRuntimeMessage` has no `PartialEq` (the existing test at `agent_sessions/tests.rs:35` already works around this via `serde_json::to_value`), so comparisons go through JSON.

```rust
use super::*;
use crate::testing::mocks::{container::ContainerMock, log::LogRepoMock};

/// NewSession -> boot -> prompt buffered -> ready -> handshake -> flush.
#[tokio::test]
async fn buffers_a_prompt_until_the_handshake_completes() {
    let id = AgentSessionId::TEST_A;
    let log = Arc::new(LogRepoMock::default());
    // No stored acp_session_id: the handshake mints one. No pump spawned.
    let session = AgentSessionImpl::new(id, ContainerMock::new(id, []), Arc::clone(&log), None);

    // Prompt lands while the container is still booting: buffered, wire silent.
    session.send_message(Some(user()), prompt("fix the flaky test")).await.unwrap();
    assert!(session.connection().sent_messages().is_empty());
    assert!(matches!(session.state(), SessionState::Booting { .. }));

    // Ready means handshakeable, not sendable: initialize goes, prompt stays.
    session.connection().push_inbound(event(SystemEvent::AcpReady));
    session.step().await.unwrap();
    assert_sent(&session, [initialize(req(1))]);
    assert!(matches!(session.state(), SessionState::Handshaking { .. }));

    // initialize answered -> session/new. Still no acp session id, still buffered.
    session.connection().push_inbound(response(req(1), json!({ "protocolVersion": 1 })));
    session.step().await.unwrap();
    assert_sent(&session, [initialize(req(1)), session_new(req(2))]);

    // session/new answered -> id recorded, buffer drains.
    session.connection().push_inbound(response(req(2), json!({ "sessionId": "acp-abc" })));
    session.step().await.unwrap();
    assert_eq!(session.acp_session_id(), Some(&AcpId::new("acp-abc")));
    assert_sent(
        &session,
        [initialize(req(1)), session_new(req(2)), prompt("fix the flaky test")],
    );

    // Live: sends pass straight through.
    session.send_message(Some(user()), prompt("and run clippy")).await.unwrap();
    assert_sent(
        &session,
        [
            initialize(req(1)),
            session_new(req(2)),
            prompt("fix the flaky test"),
            prompt("and run clippy"),
        ],
    );

    // The log is the wire transcript, in wire order.
    assert_eq!(
        log.summary(),
        [
            "<- acp_ready",
            "-> initialize",
            "<- response:1",
            "-> session/new",
            "<- response:2",
            "-> session/prompt",
            "-> session/prompt",
        ]
    );
}
```

## What this needs that we don't have

**`ContainerMock::push_inbound`** — today inbound is fixed at construction (`mocks/container.rs:21`). Seeding up front would work since request ids are deterministic, but the test stops reading chronologically. One method.

**`LogRepoMock`** — new. `append` into a `Mutex<Vec<_>>`, plus a `summary()` that projects each entry to `"<- name"` / `"-> method"`. That projection is what makes the log assertion readable instead of a wall of JSON.

**`session.step()`** — one `recv()` + handle, the manual-drive hook. `pump()` becomes `while self.step().await?  {}` and is the only untested part.

**`assert_sent`** — compares `sent_messages()` to expected via `serde_json::to_value`, since `ToRuntimeMessage` isn't `PartialEq`.

**`AgentSessionImpl::new` grows two params** — the log repo and the optional stored `acp_session_id` (`None` = `session/new`, `Some` = `session/load`).

## The thing I'd flag

This test now proves the state machine *and* the transport *and* the log wiring in one go. That's what you asked for and it's a great smoke test — but when it fails at step 4, it won't tell you whether the bug is in the buffering, the id correlation, or the log. I'd keep it as the one happy-path integration test and still put the edge cases (double `AcpReady`, send-after-disconnect, `session/load` on resume) against the bare state machine where failures are unambiguous.

Also note step 6 asserts log-at-flush — the buffered prompt appears once, at the end, not when enqueued. If you want the durable-buffer semantics instead, that line changes and `send_message` starts writing before the wire.

Want me to build this for real now? I'd go: mocks + `step()` first, then this test failing end-to-end, then fill the state machine until it passes.

It's the in-memory test double for `AgentSessionLogRepo` — the trait that already exists at `agent_session/src/domain/ports.rs:42`, the one `PgAgentSessionLogRepo` implements against postgres.

You said each session owns a log repo automatically, so constructing a session *requires* one. In tests you don't want postgres, so you pass this instead. It's also your "first pass will be a mock impl."

```rust
#[derive(Default)]
pub struct LogRepoMock {
    entries: Mutex<Vec<AgentSessionLog>>,
}

impl AgentSessionLogRepo for LogRepoMock {
    async fn create(&self, log: AgentSessionLog) -> Result<()> {
        self.entries.lock().expect("log mock poisoned").push(log);
        Ok(())
    }

    async fn list_by_session(&self, id: AgentSessionId) -> Result<Vec<AgentSessionLog>> {
        Ok(self
            .entries
            .lock()
            .expect("log mock poisoned")
            .iter()
            .filter(|e| e.agent_session_id == id)
            .cloned()
            .collect())
    }
}
```

Same shape as `ContainerMock` (`Mutex<Vec<_>>` + snapshot accessor), so it reads consistently with what's already there.

`summary()` is **not** part of the trait — it's test-only sugar that projects each entry to a one-line string so the assertion is readable:

```rust
impl LogRepoMock {
    /// "<- acp_ready", "-> initialize", ...
    pub fn summary(&self) -> Vec<String>;
}
```

Without it, asserting the transcript means comparing a `Vec<AgentSessionLog>` of nested JSON-RPC blobs, which is unreadable in a failure diff.

One wrinkle: to produce `"-> initialize"` it has to pull the `method` (or `id`) off the `RawJsonRpcMessage` inside `Message::ToRuntime(ToRuntimeMessage::Acp(AcpMessage(raw)))`. That means a `serde_json::to_value` and an index — a bit grubby, but it's test-only and it's the difference between a legible assertion and a wall of JSON.

The real value: the session is generic over the trait, so the composition root swaps `LogRepoMock` for `PgAgentSessionLogRepo` with no change to the session or its tests.

